WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.650
I asked my class how to draw a house,

2
00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.970
and they said: draw a square.

3
00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.010
So I drew a tiny square, in

4
00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:09.810
the corner of the board!

5
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.040
They laughed, but they had just

6
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.000
found out what a computer needs.

7
00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:18.790
Today: algorithms, and why

8
00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:20.790
instructions must be exact.

9
00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.430
An algorithm is a list of steps

10
00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:25.750
that solves a problem.

11
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.520
Each step is precise, the steps come

12
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.160
in order, and the list finishes.

13
00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.430
A recipe is an algorithm.

14
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:37.250
So are the directions to the market.

15
00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.390
A program is an algorithm

16
00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.710
written for a computer.

17
00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.850
The computer does exactly what each

18
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.790
step says, and nothing more.

19
00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:48.910
It can’t guess what you meant.

20
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.950
So draw a square isn’t

21
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:54.390
enough: how big, and where?

22
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.920
Algorithms are all around a school.

23
00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.410
The steps of long division.

24
00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:02.010
The fire drill.

25
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.240
Making tea for the staffroom.

26
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:07.230
Each one works only if the steps are

27
00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.110
clear and in the right order.

28
00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.350
Think of giving directions to a visitor

29
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.170
who has never been to your town.

30
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.760
Turn left at the market, but which corner?

31
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.000
Walk a little way, but how far?

32
00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.670
Good directions, like good

33
00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.950
algorithms, leave nothing to guess.

34
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:27.760
Quick check!

35
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.280
I want a robot to make tea.

36
00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.670
Which instructions can it follow?

37
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:35.840
The numbered list.

38
00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.470
A robot can’t fill the gaps

39
00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:39.530
the way a person can.

40
00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:41.930
Every step must be clear,

41
00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.410
and in the right order.

42
00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:45.530
You can’t pour the tea

43
00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:46.870
before you boil the water.

44
00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.970
Here is a robot on a grid.

45
00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.750
It understands only three commands:

46
00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:56.550
forward one square, turn left and turn

47
00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:56.770
right.

48
00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.580
Turning doesn’t move it; it only

49
00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:00.940
changes the way it faces.

50
00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.030
To reach the target, you put the

51
00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.170
commands in the exact order.

52
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.100
You don’t need a device for this.

53
00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.050
Chalk a grid on the floor, and

54
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.530
one pupil is the robot.

55
00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:15.710
The class holds up arrow

56
00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.210
cards, one at a time.

57
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.250
The robot does exactly what each card

58
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.690
says, even if it walks into a wall.

59
00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.990
In the app, open Program a robot.

60
00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.430
Lay the command cards in order, then

61
00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.410
press Run and watch it walk.

62
00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.230
Start with grids one to three.

63
00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:36.980
Your turn.

64
00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:41.590
My robot faces up, and the target is one

65
00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.410
square up and one to the right.

66
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.730
Which program gets it there?

67
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.440
Forward, turn right, forward.

68
00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.980
Remember: an algorithm is precise

69
00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.560
steps, in order, that finish.

70
00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.490
A computer does exactly what you

71
00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.450
say, not what you mean.

72
00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.640
And you can teach it all with

73
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.440
chalk, cards and a pupil robot.

74
00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:07.980
Try it in class tomorrow:

75
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:09.620
play Human robot.

76
00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.100
Chalk a grid, choose a robot, and let

77
00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.360
the class guide it to a target with

78
00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.780
arrow cards, one card at a time.

79
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.140
When it goes wrong, ask:

80
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:21.900
which card was the bug?
